# Vendor-neutral MCP stdio configuration companion

The Ansible role installed a pinned Desktop Commander MCP server but did not
install a vendor tunnel client, a long-running systemd service, or any vendor key.

1. As {{ dcmcp_user }}, run `umask 077` and copy
   `desktop-commander.json.example` to `{{ dcmcp_desktop_config }}` with `cp -n`.
2. Keep `{{ dcmcp_desktop_config }}` owned by {{ dcmcp_user }} with mode 0600;
   the parent directory must be owned by that account and mode 0700.
3. Run `playbooks/preflight.yml`, then `playbooks/activate.yml` to select the
   validated release. Neither operation starts a background daemon.
4. Configure an MCP-capable agent to execute this command as {{ dcmcp_user }}:

   `{{ dcmcp_helpers }}/mcp-child`

The launcher reserves stdout for MCP JSON-RPC, uses the selected account's
real HOME and refuses execution by another UID. It inherits ordinary filesystem,
process and sudo rights. The default JSON allows access wherever that account
is permitted; review `docs/security.md` before granting it to an agent.

For remote CLI clients, use an already-authorized non-interactive SSH session
(`ssh -T -o BatchMode=yes {{ dcmcp_user }}@YOUR_HOST {{ dcmcp_helpers }}/mcp-child`).
This is SSH-stdio, not a cloud relay. The client must be able to reach SSH.
Do not point agents at OpenAI's API hostname or reuse OpenAI tunnel credentials.
Desktop Commander retains the historical upstream `.claude-server-commander`
configuration directory name regardless of which AI agent invokes it.
